```ts
const { browser, page } = await launch();
await page.goto('https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html');

// Two XPath locators: one names a placeholder, and one names an id.
const lastName = page.locator("xpath=//input[@placeholder='Last Name']");
const submitButton = page.locator("xpath=//button[@id='submitbtn']");

// Two CSS selectors: one names a type, and one names an id.
const phone = page.locator("input[type='tel']");
const cricket = page.locator('#checkbox1');

console.log(await lastName.count());
console.log(await submitButton.count());
console.log(await phone.count());
console.log(await cricket.count());

await browser.close();
```

The Console tab shows:

```output
1
1
1
1
```

**What to check in your own answer.** Each XPath starts with `//`, and each attribute inside its square brackets starts with `@`. Neither CSS selector has an `@`, because `@` belongs to XPath. Every count is `1`, which is what a locator needs before you act on it.

